meta {
  name: HLS master playlist
  type: http
  seq: 9
}

get {
  url: {{hlsOrigin}}/{{groupPrefix}}{{streamName}}/index.m3u8
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{relayAccessToken}}
}

tests {
  test("answers a playlist", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
    expect(res.getBody()).to.contain("#EXTM3U");
  });
}

docs {
  # HLS master playlist

  One stream's master playlist, which is what a browser falling back from WebRTC opens.

  The one media leg that is a plain HTTP GET, so it is where a token is quickest to check: an authenticating relay refuses it where the token does not grant read on the path, and a relay that authenticates nothing ignores the header.
  HLS and WebRTC take the credential in a header rather than a query, their query form being a browser's and answered with a cookie and a redirect that a client keeping no cookie is refused at.

  The relay answers this route with a 302 to the same URL carrying `cookieCheck`, whether or not it authenticates, so what reaches the playlist is a client that follows redirects.

  The URL is `groupPrefix` and `streamName` joined, which is the same address in both environments: a machine in no group has an empty prefix and publishes under the bare name.

  This is the relay's own HLS listener, and the deployment's proxy routes everything it does not match to it (`deploy/Caddyfile`).
}
